# Pane layout and list typography

## Split panes → always resizable

Whenever the UI is split into side-by-side (or stacked) panes that share space, wrap them in `h_resizable` / `v_resizable` with `resizable_panel()` children so the user gets a drag handle. Do **not** hard-code fixed widths with only `border_r_1` / `border_b_1` as the divider.

```rust
// BAD — fixed split, no handle
div().flex().child(list.w(px(360.))).child(detail.flex_1())

// GOOD — draggable handle (same pattern as Git / Files)
h_resizable("history-panes")
    .child(resizable_panel().size(px(360.)).size_range(list_min..px(640.)).child(list))
    .child(resizable_panel().child(detail))
```

Set a sensible `size` + `size_range` so the handle stays usable. History list min width must keep the "History" / locale title on one line (header chrome + title advance; never below the git-graph gutter).

For three regions (list | detail | file), nest like `git-work`: outer `list | right`, inner `detail | preview`. A flat 3-panel group lets the right handle cascade into the list once the middle panel hits `size_range` min.

## List / stacked text → no glyph overlap

In multi-line list rows (title + meta, labels + captions):

- Put each text line in its **own** child `div`
- Use enough vertical gap (`gap_2` or more) between lines
- Prefer explicit `line_height(relative(1.35))` (or similar) on text rows
- Use `overflow_hidden` + `text_ellipsis` + `whitespace_nowrap` on single-line titles so they clip instead of colliding with neighbors
- Avoid collapsing padding (`py_0`) on dense rows; keep at least `py_1`

```rust
// BAD — tight stack, text can collide
div().flex().flex_col().gap_1()
    .child(div().text_sm().child(subject))
    .child(div().text_xs().child(meta))

// GOOD — clear separation + ellipsis
div().flex().flex_col().gap_2().min_w_0()
    .child(
        div()
            .text_sm()
            .line_height(relative(1.35))
            .overflow_hidden()
            .text_ellipsis()
            .whitespace_nowrap()
            .child(subject),
    )
    .child(div().text_xs().line_height(relative(1.35)).child(meta))
```
